WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my dad grew up in a small town in  eastern india named hazaribag  where life revolved around coal  schools bridges hospitals libraries were  built over generations directly by the  coal industry  coal companies even provided rides to  movie theaters  my grandfather worked here for 30 years  as a coal mining engineer  when it was time for my dad to decide on  his career  there were now two options in front of  him  coal  and now oil  he picked the latter partly because  their offices had air conditioning  if you've ever been to india over the  summer you may understand that choice  as a result of that simple decision  my family's life transformed  we moved from india to china to texas  all the while i was privileged enough to  receive an elite education  but despite that when it was time for me  to decide on my career  i also felt drawn towards energy issues  how predictable and boring  i wondered if i should distance myself  away from energy just to avoid doing  what two generations in my family had  already done  ultimately i settled on clean energy  telling myself this is different enough  from what my parents did and maybe i can  still retain some semblance of  originality  and i enjoyed it too  for the first time in my career it felt  as as though i was connected  to a mission that was broader than  myself  but deep down something still didn't  feel quite right  as clean energy transition and  sustainability became mainstream  buzzwords  it seemed as though everyone on my  linkedin feed including myself  had suddenly taken up arms in some sort  of war of biblical proportions  there was an epic battle being waged  against climate change and every  investment banker with a green bond was  a climate justice warrior  now i'm all for enthusiasm but it  started to feel a little too convenient  a little inauthentic  and frankly a little hollow  personally i felt ungrounded  the more i tethered myself to this grand  ambition of climate change the more  distant i felt  from my roots and family history  but as i was working to reconcile this  personal dilemma  i came across a broader and much more  important problem  amidst all the excitement about new  clean energy technologies and  innovations what was often never talked  about were the people actually on the  ground  i wondered as the world moved away from  fossil fuels  what would happen to communities like my  dad's hometown who depended on coal for  everything  who is looking out for them  let's take a moment to consider the life  of an average coal mine worker in a  developing economy  they have worked in the mines for 20  years  they've developed hearing impairments  from being too close to blast sites  they have respiratory illnesses from  direct exposure to coal ash day in and  day out  but despite these extreme challenges  this job is a blessing  it is virtually the only thing that  separates his family from extreme  poverty  now imagine what happens  when nearby coal plants in the  surrounding area are shut  he loses his job there's no other  industry in town  which means that the only two options  are to leave town  or try his hand as a daily wage laborer  how tragic  that the same person who bore the brunt  of all the environmental harms of coal  will now also bear the brunt of the  transition to clean energy  as countries move away from fossil fuels  millions in already vulnerable  communities will be put at serious risk  over 10 million people are dependent on  coal just in india but this is a problem  for the world  china indonesia australia west virginia  all of these places have communities  that are dependent  but let me be clear  coal does need to go  if the world has to come close to  meeting our climate change objectives we  do need to phase out fossil fuels over  the next few decades but the way in  which we manage this transition  could be the difference between  sustainable growth  and human catastrophe  now there are some who say that all  transitions have winners and losers it's  been this way forever  that might be true  but the sheer magnitude of the current  energy transition  will be disruptive like nothing we have  ever seen  and when lives are disrupted especially  in developing economies the result is  not just unemployment  it's extreme poverty  now i've also heard the argument  that there will be new clean energy jobs  to make up for the jobs that are lost in  fossil fuels and maybe even more net  more jobs will be created in the  aggregate over the long term  but human beings don't live in the  aggregate or over the long term  they live in the here and now with all  the struggles and dependencies that have  been deepened over generations  some also say that it should be the  government's responsibility to take care  of those who are left behind so that the  private sector can focus on innovation  and technology  but that's simply too convenient just  relying on the government to take care  of social consequences is not a strategy  it's a feel-good exercise  we need to move beyond wishful thinking  and think holistically about how our  energy transition can not just be clean  but also just  now no two communities will have the  same solution  for some it could be a direct swap of  fossil jobs to clean energy jobs  for many others like my dad's hometown  entirely new industries have to be  created whether it's eco tourism  agriculture or construction  but here's the deal  investing in the communities that will  be left behind is not just ethical  it's practical  these same communities represent much of  the opposition to clean energy  legislation today  so investing in investing in them  supporting them and making sure they're  not left behind actually maximizes our  chances of achieving the transition in  the first place  now there's a lot of work that's left to  be done  but there are some important early signs  of progress for example the just  transition initiative and the climate  justice alliance works with companies  all around the world  to pursue climate change mitigation but  also support marginalized communities  all the way from south africa to india  but if you want to make a real dent in  the problem  our efforts have to be bolder and  broader  this is about a lot more than just coal  or any other energy source this is about  about how we think of sustainability in  the first place  sustainability must be broader than just  protecting the environment it must  include protecting people  especially those who are most at risk  now companies and governments are  certainly important but i believe that  you and i have the most important role  to play  we know that millennials and gen zers  are the most likely to make purchasing  decisions based on sustainable business  practices  so your pocketbook  can actually be a vote  an expression of solidarity with the  voices who are not yet at the table  and as future business leaders  it's our opportunity and responsibility  to ensure that those same voices also  belong in the board room  so the next time you're stepping out for  your morning coffee or deciding what  stocks to back on robin hood or  preparing for the next meeting in the  board room  push yourself  to take that extra step  challenge yourself to do a little extra  research  ask yourself  how can i support a world that is not  just more clean  but more just  as we scramble to reduce our carbon  footprint  what can i do for those who will be left  behind  thank you  [Music]

